\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The Five-Layer Instrument Stack.} (\textbf  {A})~Layer~1 hardware oscillators on a logarithmic frequency axis, each labelled with the partition coordinate it resolves: CPU clock $\sim 10^{9}$~Hz $\to n$, memory bus $\sim 10^{8}$~Hz $\to \ell $, LED emission $\sim 10^{14}$~Hz $\to m$, memory refresh $\sim 10^{4}$~Hz $\to s$. The four oscillator subsystems span ten orders of magnitude, matching the analyte's range from atomic transitions to slow conformational reorganisation. (\textbf  {B})~Layer~3 ensemble strobe windows on a logarithmic timescale axis. The three temporal gates are clocked by the analyte's own absorption and emission events: $W_{S_k}$ at femtoseconds (absorption phase, hop~1 hydride transfer), $W_{S_t}$ at nanoseconds (excited-state lifetime, hop~2 semiquinone-mediated transfer), $W_{S_e}$ at $\mu $s+ (decay, hop~3 long-range tunnelling). The three timescales are separated by $\geq 10^{3}$ in ratio, giving orthogonality across the chain. (\textbf  {C})~Layer~5 hologram pipeline observable list. Six observables emerge from the diffraction pattern; observable~5 (Marcus reorganisation energy $\lambda $, highlighted green) is the headline numerical observable for this paper. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional pyramidal rendering of the five-layer stack, bottom-up: oscillators (L1) $\to $ equivalence certificate (L2) $\to $ strobes (L3) $\to $ harmonic resonator (L4) $\to $ hologram pipeline (L5). Each layer's output feeds the next; the pyramid form encodes the increasing abstraction from raw oscillator phase-locks to high-level molecular observables.}}{12}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:apparatus_stack}{{6}{12}{\textbf {The Five-Layer Instrument Stack.} (\textbf {A})~Layer~1 hardware oscillators on a logarithmic frequency axis, each labelled with the partition coordinate it resolves: CPU clock $\sim 10^{9}$~Hz $\to n$, memory bus $\sim 10^{8}$~Hz $\to \ell $, LED emission $\sim 10^{14}$~Hz $\to m$, memory refresh $\sim 10^{4}$~Hz $\to s$. The four oscillator subsystems span ten orders of magnitude, matching the analyte's range from atomic transitions to slow conformational reorganisation. (\textbf {B})~Layer~3 ensemble strobe windows on a logarithmic timescale axis. The three temporal gates are clocked by the analyte's own absorption and emission events: $W_{S_k}$ at femtoseconds (absorption phase, hop~1 hydride transfer), $W_{S_t}$ at nanoseconds (excited-state lifetime, hop~2 semiquinone-mediated transfer), $W_{S_e}$ at $\mu $s+ (decay, hop~3 long-range tunnelling). The three timescales are separated by $\geq 10^{3}$ in ratio, giving orthogonality across the chain. (\textbf {C})~Layer~5 hologram pipeline observable list. Six observables emerge from the diffraction pattern; observable~5 (Marcus reorganisation energy $\lambda $, highlighted green) is the headline numerical observable for this paper. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional pyramidal rendering of the five-layer stack, bottom-up: oscillators (L1) $\to $ equivalence certificate (L2) $\to $ strobes (L3) $\to $ harmonic resonator (L4) $\to $ hologram pipeline (L5). Each layer's output feeds the next; the pyramid form encodes the increasing abstraction from raw oscillator phase-locks to high-level molecular observables}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\newlabel{fig:apparatus_stack@cref}{{[figure][6][]6}{[1][11][]12}{}{}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Cofactor Self-Selection by Counting-Anomaly $\chi ^2$ Test.} (\textbf  {A})~$\chi ^2$ statistic computed for each of the four candidate cofactor centres (NADPH-C4, FAD-N5, FMN-N5, heme-Fe) against the equilibrium ternary state distribution. All four exceed the $\chi ^2_{\mathrm  {threshold}} = 5.99$ (red dashed line, 95\% CI for 2 dof), self-identifying as electron-transfer-active centres without any prior specification by the experimenter. The bystander mean (grey dotted) lies far below threshold. (\textbf  {B})~Equilibrium versus electron-transfer-active ternary state distributions. Equilibrium (grey, bystanders) approximately reproduces the lysozyme baseline of \citep  {sachikonye2025_atomicspec} (0.26/0.50/0.24); ET-active centres (green, cofactors) skew strongly toward $\tau = 2$ (excited state) during the transfer, generating the $\chi ^2$ anomaly that drives self-selection. (\textbf  {C})~Selection metrics: cofactor recall ($100\%$, all four cofactors selected) and selection specificity (1 minus false-positive rate, $\geq 85\%$). The metrics replicate the precedent set by azurin Cu-site detection in \citep  {sachikonye2025_atomicspec}. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional view of the $\chi ^2$ landscape over the cofactor cluster. The four cofactor positions (green spheres, labelled NADPH/FAD/FMN/Fe) sit above the red threshold plane; bystander atoms (grey dots) are scattered below. The threshold plane is the operational definition of ``active atom'' --- atoms above the plane self-select as participants in the electron transfer event.}}{12}{figure.caption.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:counting_anomaly}{{7}{12}{\textbf {Cofactor Self-Selection by Counting-Anomaly $\chi ^2$ Test.} (\textbf {A})~$\chi ^2$ statistic computed for each of the four candidate cofactor centres (NADPH-C4, FAD-N5, FMN-N5, heme-Fe) against the equilibrium ternary state distribution. All four exceed the $\chi ^2_{\mathrm {threshold}} = 5.99$ (red dashed line, 95\% CI for 2 dof), self-identifying as electron-transfer-active centres without any prior specification by the experimenter. The bystander mean (grey dotted) lies far below threshold. (\textbf {B})~Equilibrium versus electron-transfer-active ternary state distributions. Equilibrium (grey, bystanders) approximately reproduces the lysozyme baseline of \citep {sachikonye2025_atomicspec} (0.26/0.50/0.24); ET-active centres (green, cofactors) skew strongly toward $\tau = 2$ (excited state) during the transfer, generating the $\chi ^2$ anomaly that drives self-selection. (\textbf {C})~Selection metrics: cofactor recall ($100\%$, all four cofactors selected) and selection specificity (1 minus false-positive rate, $\geq 85\%$). The metrics replicate the precedent set by azurin Cu-site detection in \citep {sachikonye2025_atomicspec}. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional view of the $\chi ^2$ landscape over the cofactor cluster. The four cofactor positions (green spheres, labelled NADPH/FAD/FMN/Fe) sit above the red threshold plane; bystander atoms (grey dots) are scattered below. The threshold plane is the operational definition of ``active atom'' --- atoms above the plane self-select as participants in the electron transfer event}{figure.caption.9}{}}
\newlabel{fig:counting_anomaly@cref}{{[figure][7][]7}{[1][11][]12}{}{}{}}
