\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Hologram Observable~\#5 (Marcus $\lambda $) and Femtosecond-Resolved Electron-Movement Visualisations --- HEADLINE.} (\textbf  {A})~Marcus reorganisation energy $\lambda $ recovery: target value (x-axis) versus value recovered from the hologram diffraction-peak Gaussian width via $\lambda = \sigma ^{2} / (2 k_B T)$ (y-axis). All three test values across the literature range 0.7--1.0~eV recover within the $\pm 20\%$ band (green shading). The black dashed line is ideal recovery; deviations are within the apparatus's nominal noise floor of 5\% per axis. (\textbf  {B})~Electron-position centroid along the four-cofactor chain (NADPH at 0~\r A, FAD at 4~\r A, FMN at 8~\r A, heme at 26~\r A) at five frame times $t \in \{0, 100, 250, 500, 800\}$~fs. The plasma colourmap traces temporal progression: at $t = 0$~fs the electron is localised on NADPH; by $t = 800$~fs it has been delivered to the heme. The trajectory crosses the three intercofactor gaps in order, confirming the sequential hop structure. (\textbf  {C})~$|\psi (\mathbf  {r}, t)|^{2}$ probability-density profiles along the chain at the five frame times, stacked vertically with the time stamp on the right. Cofactor positions are marked by grey dotted lines. The density envelope at $t = 0$~fs is sharply peaked at NADPH; by $t = 800$~fs it is centred on the heme with a broader spread reflecting the longer FMN $\to $ heme tunnelling distance. (\textbf  {D})~Three-dimensional voxel-style rendering of $|\psi (\mathbf  {r}, t)|^{2}$ at the mid-frame $t = 500$~fs in the FMN-flavodoxin / heme-pocket region. The plasma-coloured cloud is the GPU pipeline's per-voxel density readout; the four cofactor markers (NADPH blue, FAD orange, FMN green, heme red) anchor the spatial reference. This panel is the headline visual deliverable: the electron's instantaneous position in the protein matrix, observed by the apparatus stack rather than predicted by a kinetic model.}}{14}{figure.caption.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:electron_visualisations}{{8}{14}{\textbf {Hologram Observable~\#5 (Marcus $\lambda $) and Femtosecond-Resolved Electron-Movement Visualisations --- HEADLINE.} (\textbf {A})~Marcus reorganisation energy $\lambda $ recovery: target value (x-axis) versus value recovered from the hologram diffraction-peak Gaussian width via $\lambda = \sigma ^{2} / (2 k_B T)$ (y-axis). All three test values across the literature range 0.7--1.0~eV recover within the $\pm 20\%$ band (green shading). The black dashed line is ideal recovery; deviations are within the apparatus's nominal noise floor of 5\% per axis. (\textbf {B})~Electron-position centroid along the four-cofactor chain (NADPH at 0~\AA , FAD at 4~\AA , FMN at 8~\AA , heme at 26~\AA ) at five frame times $t \in \{0, 100, 250, 500, 800\}$~fs. The plasma colourmap traces temporal progression: at $t = 0$~fs the electron is localised on NADPH; by $t = 800$~fs it has been delivered to the heme. The trajectory crosses the three intercofactor gaps in order, confirming the sequential hop structure. (\textbf {C})~$|\psi (\mathbf {r}, t)|^{2}$ probability-density profiles along the chain at the five frame times, stacked vertically with the time stamp on the right. Cofactor positions are marked by grey dotted lines. The density envelope at $t = 0$~fs is sharply peaked at NADPH; by $t = 800$~fs it is centred on the heme with a broader spread reflecting the longer FMN $\to $ heme tunnelling distance. (\textbf {D})~Three-dimensional voxel-style rendering of $|\psi (\mathbf {r}, t)|^{2}$ at the mid-frame $t = 500$~fs in the FMN-flavodoxin / heme-pocket region. The plasma-coloured cloud is the GPU pipeline's per-voxel density readout; the four cofactor markers (NADPH blue, FAD orange, FMN green, heme red) anchor the spatial reference. This panel is the headline visual deliverable: the electron's instantaneous position in the protein matrix, observed by the apparatus stack rather than predicted by a kinetic model}{figure.caption.11}{}}
\newlabel{fig:electron_visualisations@cref}{{[figure][8][]8}{[1][13][]14}{}{}{}}
